But the square on *D* is to the rectangle *D* by *E* as *D* is to *E,* therefore *A* is to *C* as *D* is to *E.* But *A* is commensurable with *C* in square only, therefore *D* is also commensurable with *E* in square only. [!just X.11]

But *D* is medial, therefore *E* is also medial. <div class="just"><a href="/elements/books/bookX/propositions/propX23/#note">X.23,Note</a></div>

And, since *A* is to *C* as *D* is to *E,* while the square on *A* is greater than the square on *C* by the square on a straight line commensurable with *A,* therefore the square on *D* is greater than the square on *E* by the square on a straight line commensurable with *D.* [!just X.14]

I say next that the rectangle *D* by *E* is also medial.

Since the rectangle *B* by *C* equals the rectangle *D* by *E,* while the rectangle *B* by *C* is medial, therefore the rectangle *D* by *E* is also medial. [!just X.21]

Therefore two medial straight lines *D* and *E,* commensurable in square only, and containing a medial rectangle, have been found such that the square on the greater is greater than the square on the less by the square on a straight line commensurable with the greater.

Similarly again it can be proved that the square on *D* is greater than the square on *E* by the square on a straight line incommensurable with *D* when the square on *A* is greater than the square on *C* by the square on a straight line incommensurable with *A.* [!just X.30]

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
----
guide: This proposition is not used in the rest of the *Elements.*
